            <ArticleTitle>The relation of warmth and pain thresholds to skin pigmentation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Thermal sensitivity increases with skin pigmentation. Absolute thresholds for warmth and pricking pain decreased as skin color increased for 13 Caucasian, 3 Mexican, and 4 Negro subjects. These data replicate and extend a previous psychophysical study of pain sensitivity and are compared with spectrophotometric data from excised skin. The results are considered to be potentially significant for the existence of a dermal light sense.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
